RESOLUTION

What’s Happening

Your system is trying to use this as DNS:

192.168.0.1


But that device (usually your router) is refusing DNS requests.

So:

❌ Your router is not acting as a DNS server
❌ Or DNS forwarding is broken
❌ Or firewall is blocking port 53

That’s why all recon tools are failing.


✅ Immediate Fix (Do This Now)

Bypass your router and use public DNS.

Step 1: Edit resolv.conf
sudo nano /etc/resolv.conf


Delete everything inside and paste:

nameserver 8.8.8.8
nameserver 1.1.1.1

Save → Exit.


Step 2: Lock It (Prevent Auto-Overwrite)

Some systems reset this file. Run:

sudo chattr +i /etc/resolv.conf


(This makes it immutable.)

⚠️ Later, if you want to edit it again:

sudo chattr -i /etc/resolv.conf


Step 3: Restart Network

sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager


Or just reboot.


🧪 Step 4: Test

After that, test:

nslookup google.com
ping google.com
curl https://api.hackertarget.com


You should now see:

✅ DNS resolves
✅ Internet works
✅ Recon-ng works


